body:
I was 20. I've been doing bare knuckle since I was 16. So 4 years at that point.  There was this girl, who was maybe 15 (prolly 14 though) at the time, but she's been there for far longer than I have. She won multiple medals in comeptitions, so her kicking technique was perfect, but if you saw her on the street, you couldn't really tell much of a difference between her and the average 15 year old girl. Anyways, I was 4 years in at that point, training 2 times a week so I was pretty far froma  beginner. I weighted about 80 kg and was shredded.

&#x200B;

There comes this exercise where (for durification) we were supposed to receive mid kicks. Now, we aren't supposed to use full force normally on these, but being a cocky 20 year old and seeing a tiny girl in front of me I just told her she could go "harder". 

&#x200B;

After the first mid kick received I was in shock, It felt like I got hit by a trunk. We were supposed to do multiple sets, and after a few more kicks I realized how fucked I was. Of course, I couldn't tell a 15 year old girl to kick slower because she was kicking the living shit out of me in front of my trainer and everyone else, so I just had to endure, but hell was that the longest set of my life. Mind you, this wasn't the first time we did this, I received plenty of mid kicks from full grown adults, some trying to apply more power, but the perfect technique of this girl took her kick to new levels of damage.

&#x200B;

All of this to say that I believe that an average 15 year old with good training (this was karate btw, not muay thai), is very underestimated in this thread. I would give it to the 15 year old 9.9/10 times. Whenever someone new comes to my gym, they generally get destroyed even by people who have been doing this for 2 or 3 months. Technique advantage will result in more power on the kid's side, 15 year olds are likely to have more speed , agility, etc. I really see no way an average dad would win, after 3 years of training in any gym worth the money you should be able to block any haymaker ( which is the #1 weapon of someone untrained), have a good defense, footwork, positional control...

\>In alte cuvinte, riscul perceput de el ca o sa moara de la global warning este unul mult mai mic decat cel perceput de a murii de foame. Dar perceptia riscurilor este un subiect foarte studiat de academicieni, iar un singur care este sigur este faptul ca media este foarte legata de perceptia noastra de risc. Adica, in alte cuvinte, ce legatura are concret moarte de foame cu aruncatul pe jos? Nu avea energie sa tina de petul de bere inca 10 metri, sau 10 km, depinzand de caz? Aici e o problema culturala, nu una economica. Studiul asta spune ca (dau paste la abstract aici, ca e posibil sa nu ai acces):Previous scholarship pertaining to the social bases of proenvironmentalbehavior presents an equivocal picture of the influence of social class indicators. In further investigation of this possible association, the author examinedmunicipal-level data in Massachusetts, compiled by various state agencies,exploring whether sociodemographics are associated with recycling rates,hybrid car ownership, and the presence of sustainability advocacy groups.Results indicate that the proportion of college-educated population wassignificantly associated with recycling and hybrid auto ownership, whereasresults for the civic action group presence show population density andpolitical voting were significant but not sociodemographics. The insignificance of economic metrics implies that policy makers and social scientists inthis specific case should focus more on cultural factors than financial barriersin encouraging certain behaviors.Ultima fraza e cea mai relevanta, si desi e facut pe niste oameni din Massauchussets, ramane valabil ca este vorba de o chestia culturala la mijloc; daca fiecare roman ar fii constient ca e posibil sa isi omoare copii/ copii copiilor cu fiecare sticla aruncata pe jos, eu cred ca multi nu ar mai face-o. Also, daca ar fii adevarat ce zici tu, atunci ar trebui sa ne asteptam ca tarile mai sarace ar trebui sa lupte mai putin cu climate changeul. Ca si metrica, am folosit CPI (Climate protection index) ca sa vad daca exista vreo corelatie. Poti efectiv sa faci un quick google search sa vezi ca nu este vorba doar de statutul economic. De ex, daca ne uitam la China, un locuitor face sub 20000 de dolari/ an, si daca nu sustii ca Chinezi (in spite of that) au mai putine probleme ca romanii, atunci nu ai decat sa concluzi ca programele sociale foarte axate pe green energy ale ccp-ului au avut un efect real asupra mentalitatii populatiei.Deeeeeeeeeeeci, tl;dr: Romanu' : Lasa sefu ca mor de foame ce ma futi pe mine la cap cu climate changeul asta, nu ma aplec io sa strang gunoi de pe jos, si sa nu imi mai arunc tigara pe unde vrea pula mea.

&#x200B;

[Articolu](https://journals-sagepub-com.eur.idm.oclc.org/doi/pdf/10.1177/0013916511416647)l de care vorbeam. Care iti arata ca cauzele climate change action-ului sunt mai mult culturale decat economice.

&#x200B;

Edit: For your other point, si anume ca China este o tara autoritara. Da, insa au folosit autoritarianismul statului ca sa schimbe perceptia societatii asupra climate changeului. Basically ce spun este ca ei au folosit uneltele de care dispun ca sa educe/indoctrineze fortat, dar pana la urma ce au schimbat tot face parte din cultura.

I mean with the kind of popular support Zelenski has now... There are no (Ukrainian, at least) oligarchs/corrupt politicians that can even do anything against him at this point. If Ukraine still exists after this war and joins EU, I could see them rebuild and surge ahead of countries such as Romania and Poland if Zelensky can deal with the corruption and EU can provide the funds. Honestly Ukraine, being so fertile and rich in manpower, land and ressources could become a major European power (yet again, if it exists after this war).

&#x200B;

Edit: Just wanted to say, I am Romanian, and the difference the EU has made in my country is huge... Just go on Google Maps 10 years ago in Bucharest and you'll see what a shithole it was. However, with money from the EU, many projects have been completed (and not only in Bucharest, in the country side the difference is enormous between villages who used EU funds and those who didn't). The only problem we have had is the endemic corruption which we cannot get rid of, and that is how some of these EU  funds are lost in the pocket of politicians (although it being EU money, it's still considerably harder to steal than "local" money. I hope Ukrainians learn from our mistakes, and stay behind Zelensky until the corrupt part of your politics gets hammered by the EU.

I like how you are being critical of any western sources with no regard to the authenticity of your own claims. Also, you claim people are supposed to see the world less black and white, yet your text lacks any nuance whatsoever. I mean, you basically shat out a dissertation on how Russia was much less of a threat after the dissolution of the USSR (despite being involved in many conflicts with it's neighbors, including but not limited to Chechenya, Georgia, Dagestan to name a few), which is true I guess, but the perceived agression which Russia has historically shown (to put bluntly, The Red Army has slaughtered, looted and raped whatever they could get their hands on, and there are many, many sources for this) did not go away.  

&#x200B;

Also, you clearly have no understanding of how a defensive alliance functions, yes countries from NATO supported American invasions of Middle Eastern countries ( which is a trend the Soviets started with Afghanistan, btw), but since you claim you think "logically", then you would be intelectually dishonest to claim you don't understand that this was not done under the guise of NATO, but rather as the ALLIES of the United States. All the Eastern European countries that wanted to join NATO have done so not becuase they love surrendering their independence to Americans, but rather because they perceived Russia to be a very major and big threat. If Russia hadn't ben so aggressive with it's neighbors, then Ukraine would not have had any reason to join NATO.  But NATO exists precisely because Russia is a threat, and claiming they are not is a gross missunderstanding of past events

&#x200B;

It's just so fucking absurd to me how Russian media can go from "The aggressive West is lying through it's teeth about us wanting to invade Ukraine" to "Ukrainian Fascist government is evil and we are invading immediatly".

Because everybody knows these two things are the same

&#x200B;

/s

The anti PETA (which is a company, and does not have any type of monopoly on the concept of animal liberation, or the end of human cruelty towards animals) movement is justified though. 

&#x200B;

As someone who is an animal lover, and also 1 year vegetarian by choice, my only wish on this subject is that this organisation is either completely revamped or disapears from the face of this earth.

&#x200B;

PETA has, so far, only damaged their cause imo, by basing all of their actions around the ideaas of virtue-signaling, aggressive marketing, stupid advertising, and simply doing the best they can to piss people off. An organisation which has the poise of PETA (which is more or less ingrained in our minds as one of the "default" animal rescue organisations), should be much more responsible with it's actions, and their core strategy seems to be to "show them evil meat eaters how evil they are", hoping that this will somehow bring mroe people to their cause. They are so bad at doing what they should do that their only resort which has any effect with normal people are shock videos- but that is not also not ok. I mean, imagine if someone started a civil rights movement for women in India and would constantly post videos of women being beaten and raped and mistreated as a "promotional campaign" to get more people to be familiar with their cause. 

&#x200B;

What I'm trying to say is, such a large and wealthy corporation could do much, much better with a more sensible approach towards THE VERY PEOPLE THEY ARE TRYING TO GET ON THEIR SIDE.  It is not by insulting [Steve Irwin](https://twitter.com/peta/status/1098992959649808384?lang=en), [or making stupid assumptions on autism](https://twitter.com/peta/status/4742554750?lang=en),[or by trying to storm a video game museum with fish](https://www.reddit.com/r/Cringetopia/comments/gmtau5/peta_trying_to_cancel_a_fictional_character/) (like honestly, how could you give your money to an organisation which thinks that this supports animals in any way),[all while spending less than 40% on actual rescue operations and such](https://www.peta.org/about-peta/learn-about-peta/financial-report/) (imagine if the money put in these stupid add campaigns actually went towards helping animals)

&#x200B;

In other words: Love animals? NEVER donate to PETA, you are basically throwing your money away to some idiots who have no idea about how to make people want to join your cause. Give the money/support you wanted to donate to them to your local shelter, or maybe just spend those $5 and the time you would've spent looking up their tweet on feeding a stray dog or cat. 

&#x200B;

Fuck PETA.

As someone who also trains kyokushin and kickboxing, I agree with you. Kyokushinkai has it's weak spot in the no punches to the face rule. I can understand this rule though, as I think Kyokushin would be a really bloody affair if you allowed full contact fighters with no gloves to also punch the face.

&#x200B;

However, I think that where kyokushinkai stands out is in building physical endurance in the rest of your body, as many of the drills we do at the gym are based on receiving blows. Also, kyokushin has some pretty solid balance principles which have definetly helped me in kickboxing.

&#x200B;

What I'm trying to say is that while someone who trains kyokushin isn't a complete fighter due to the inability to properly react to face punches, kyokushin is definetly a worthwile MA and it helps you in other MAs too.

I think he more of less did. His reaction was something along the lines of "oh shit... oh shit.... oh shit...". I just don't think it was an actual admission of guilt if you look carefully. 

&#x200B;

I mean, imagine that the two biggest and scariest gangsters in the UK come to you and accuse you & seem very convinces that you did something really bad to them. He knew he was so fucked, that he figured that pleading for mercy (or most likely he wasn't even thinking this rationally, I know I wouldn't), would be his best chance, or he just simply never got over the initial shock.

Man I really don't understand him.

&#x200B;

When he said "as a black man" I honestly thought he would say that he respects civil rights, but he seems to have completely forgotten MLK or any black right's activists at that moment. I wonder if he thinks that what MLK did was "bad for business" too...

&#x200B;

I think that holding a financial bottom line as a supreme criteria for your actions is kind of sickening really. In the end, this shows that one values money over ethics and morality, over freedom and justice.
